      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The land that became Palatine was originally bought by William Haymond Sr. for just $140.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wvgw.net/marion/towns/palatinehis.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Palatine sits within Marion County, whose current borders were drawn in 1842 and which made Middletown the county seat.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairmont%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area that is now Palatine lies east of the Monongahela River, near the early settlement that would grow into Fairmont.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wv-roots.org/wp/2014/02/21/palatine-marion-county/",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fairmont, the county seat, was founded in 1819 as Middletown before adopting its current name in 1854.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairmont%2C_West_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Palatine is part of the Morgantown–Fairmont combined statistical area, linking it to a larger regional economy.",
